<p>A charter guest watching a yacht video wants to answer a few simple questions. What does the boat look like? Where will we spend the day? Where will we sleep? A good video answers them in the order a guest would ask them, and does it in about a minute. Here is a running order that works, and how to fit it into 60 to 90 seconds.</p>

<h2>What should a yacht charter video show?</h2>
<p>The same spaces a guest will use, roughly in the order they will use them:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Exterior and underway.</strong> The yacht on the water, ideally moving. This is the opening shot: it shows the size, the shape and the type of boat at a glance.</li>
  <li><strong>At anchor.</strong> The yacht in a bay or a calm setting. This is the picture guests have in mind when they book a charter.</li>
  <li><strong>Deck.</strong> Cockpit, sun pads, flybridge and the dining table outside. On many charters this is where most of the day is spent.</li>
  <li><strong>Saloon.</strong> The main living space inside: seating, table, light from the windows.</li>
  <li><strong>Galley.</strong> Where meals are made. Guests on bareboat charters in particular want to see it.</li>
  <li><strong>Cabins.</strong> The master cabin first, then the others. Guests compare beds and space here.</li>
  <li><strong>Bathroom.</strong> One shot is often enough, but leaving it out raises questions.</li>
  <li><strong>Details.</strong> A set table, a view through a hatch, a helm station, a swim platform. Short moments that make the boat feel looked after.</li>
</ol>
<p>A closing shot outside, at anchor or underway, brings the viewer back to the water at the end.</p>

<h2>Why this order?</h2>
<p>It follows the way a guest imagines the trip: arriving, spending the day on deck, going inside, going to sleep. Starting outside also matters for attention. A boat on blue water is easier to recognise and more likely to make someone keep watching than an interior.</p>
<p>Jumping back and forth between deck and cabins makes the layout hard to follow. Keeping each area together lets the viewer build a picture of the boat as they watch.</p>

<h2>How long should each part be?</h2>
<p>For a 60 to 90 second film, a rough split looks like this:</p>
<div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Rough running order for a 60 to 90 second yacht charter video" tabindex="0">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Part</th><th scope="col">Rough length</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td data-label="Part">Exterior, underway and at anchor</td><td data-label="Rough length">10–15 s</td><td data-label="What it does">Shows the boat and sets the scene</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Part">Deck and flybridge</td><td data-label="Rough length">10–20 s</td><td data-label="What it does">Where the day is spent</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Part">Saloon and galley</td><td data-label="Rough length">10–15 s</td><td data-label="What it does">Living space inside</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Part">Cabins and bathroom</td><td data-label="Rough length">20–25 s</td><td data-label="What it does">Where guests sleep, compared closely</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Part">Details and closing shot</td><td data-label="Rough length">10–15 s</td><td data-label="What it does">Character and a calm ending</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>These are guidelines, not rules. A large motor yacht with a flybridge and five cabins needs more time inside; a small sailing yacht may spend more time on deck. The pacing follows what the photos show.</p>

<h2>Why keep it calm?</h2>
<p>Slow, steady camera moves let the viewer look at the space. Fast moves feel restless and make a boat look smaller. In a video made with AI from photos, calm moves also give a more reliable result: large changes of angle make the software guess, and guesses can look wrong. We keep the movement slow on purpose.</p>

<h2>What if some of these photos are missing?</h2>
<p>The video can only show what is in your photos. If the yacht was never photographed underway, or the flybridge or a cabin is missing from the set, it will not be in the film. We do not add people, views or features that are not there, and there is no recorded sound.</p>
<p>Most charter listings already have exterior, deck, saloon, galley and cabin photos, which is enough for a full walkthrough. If one area is missing, the film works around it. If it matters to you, a new photo of that area is the simplest fix.</p>

<h2>16:9 or 9:16: does the order change?</h2>
<p>The running order stays the same, but the framing changes.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>16:9 (landscape)</strong> suits your website, listing platforms and YouTube. A wide frame fits the length of a hull and the width of a saloon, so it is the more natural format for showing the whole boat.</li>
  <li><strong>9:16 (vertical)</strong> suits Instagram Reels, TikTok and Stories, where people watch on a phone held upright. A vertical frame crops the sides, so each shot focuses on part of a space: the bow, a bed, a helm, a doorway. Details work well here.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both formats are included with every video, so you do not have to choose. You get MP4 files in 16:9 and 9:16, ready to upload.</p>

<h2>How does this work for a fleet?</h2>
<p>For a fleet, it helps if every yacht follows the same running order and the same calm style. A guest comparing boats on your website can then compare like with like. Each yacht gets its own video, and we make up to 2 videos a day, with the delivery date agreed beforehand.</p>
